<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">British occupation of Manila</h1><p class="st-meta">1762 CE – 1764 CE · capital Manila</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Brief British rule of Manila during the Seven Years War, which exposed the weakness of Spanish power in the Philippines.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years War</a>, a British expedition from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-draper">William Draper</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-cornish">Samuel Cornish</a> attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> in September 1762. The city fell on 6 October, and the acting governor, Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-rojo-del-rio-y-vieyra">Manuel Rojo</a>, agreed a capitulation and a large ransom that was never fully paid.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>British control barely extended beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> and Cavite. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dawsonne-drake">Dawsonne Drake</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> governed as deputy, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-de-anda-y-salazar">Simón de Anda y Salazar</a> organised Spanish resistance from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bacolor">Bacolor</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pampanga">Pampanga</a>. The occupation encouraged uprisings, notably that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-silang">Diego Silang</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilocos">Ilocos</a>, who sought British support and was assassinated in 1763.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1763">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1763 returned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and the British left in April 1764. The episode prompted Spanish reforms and fortification, and revolts it had encouraged foreshadowed later Filipino resistance to Spanish rule.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/british-occupation-of-manila.jpg" alt="British occupation of Manila" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: Hoshie · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Great_Britain_(1707%E2%80%931800).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1762 CE – 1764 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Manila</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Dawsonne Drake</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish, English, Tagalog</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Philippines</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>British Manila</dd></dl></aside></div>
